In this lesson we'll look at how to make and test conjectures, which is really just a fancy way of saying guess a rule from a pattern, then check if it actually holds. So here's the idea. A conjecture is your best guess that something is true, like "every time I add one to x, y grows by the same amount." You don't just write the guess down and stop, though. You test it on new data. If your guess survives lots of tests, it graduates into a theorem. But here's the powerful part. You only need a single counter-example, one case where it fails, to throw the whole conjecture out. Now, where do linear functions fit in? Whenever a quantity changes by the same amount each step, the relation is linear. Going forward one step adds a constant. Going back several steps subtracts that constant multiple times. And here's a handy trick. A graphing app or spreadsheet lets you tweak the slope and the intercept and watch the line move, which is the fastest way to build a feel for what each part actually does. Now let's see it in action.